title: Llama Sans
---

<script>
	import { specimen } from '#lib/specimen.ts';
	import { view, nearest, variantNotes } from '#lib/view.svelte.ts';
	import Masthead from '#lib/components/Masthead.svelte';
	import Metrics from '#lib/components/Metrics.svelte';
	import Controls from '#lib/components/Controls.svelte';
	import Tester from '#lib/components/Tester.svelte';
	import InUse from '#lib/components/InUse.svelte';
	import SmallSizes from '#lib/components/SmallSizes.svelte';
	import KerningProof from '#lib/components/KerningProof.svelte';
	import Waterfall from '#lib/components/Waterfall.svelte';
	import Setting from '#lib/components/Setting.svelte';
	import MonoSample from '#lib/components/MonoSample.svelte';
	import GlyphGrid from '#lib/components/GlyphGrid.svelte';
	import Notes from '#lib/components/Notes.svelte';
	import Note from '#lib/components/Note.svelte';
	import CompareSwitch from '#lib/components/CompareSwitch.svelte';

	const { axis, glyphCount, styles } = specimen;
	const stems = styles.map((s) => s.stem);
</script>

<Masthead variant={view.variant}>

The sans of the Llama family, with Mono below and Serif to follow. A neo-grotesk with an even stroke, round bowls and open, angle-cut terminals, in one variable font from {axis.min} to {axis.max} with an optical size axis: Display at its large end for headlines, UI at 16 for interfaces and running text.

</Masthead>

<Metrics />

<Controls bind:weight={view.weight} bind:variant={view.variant} bind:size={view.size} bind:tracking={view.tracking} bind:guides={view.guides} />

<Tester {glyphCount} size={view.size} tracking={view.tracking} />

## In use · Llama Sans {view.variant}

{variantNotes[view.variant]}

<InUse variant={view.variant} {glyphCount} guides={view.guides} />

## Small sizes

Under 16 px the optical size axis sets the letters further apart, more the smaller the text, so they stay separate on a coarse pixel grid. Each line takes its optical size from its font-size, as a browser does.

<SmallSizes comparing={view.comparing} />

## Kerning

The pairs that go wrong first, in headlines and short sentences. Click a line to edit it.

<KerningProof />

## Weights

<Waterfall />

## Setting

<Setting weight={view.weight} />

## Llama Mono

The monospaced member of the family, built from Geist Mono with Llama Sans’s changes: the same weights, the tailless a, the narrow t, angle-cut terminals, rounder curves, deeper joins and UI’s descenders. Every glyph keeps its 600-unit cell; the l keeps its serifs, and two t’s join their crossbars across the cells.

<MonoSample weight={view.weight} />

<GlyphGrid weight={nearest(view.weight)} comparing={view.comparing} />

## Design notes

<Notes>
	<Note term="Stroke">Near-monoline, with a {stems[0]}-unit stem at 400 rising to {stems.at(-1)} at 700. Where a bowl, arm or shoulder leaves a stem, the notch cuts a little deeper into the joint, so joins stay open without turning into ink traps.</Note>
	<Note term="Curves">Bowls are eased toward true circles, fully circular in G, S and s, so rounds read soft and calm without turning geometric.</Note>
	<Note term="Terminals">Every curved stroke end is trimmed the same short distance and cut almost square to the stroke, which keeps c, e, g, s, 2, 3, 5, 6 and 9 open and consistent.</Note>
	<Note term="a">Double-story, on a straight stem with a flat foot. The hook runs long and ends in a flat cut; the bowl rises gently into the stem with sharp joints. Toward Bold, a small bracketed foot appears.</Note>
	<Note term="l, r, t, f">A plain l the width of the i, and a narrow t with short arms. The ends of r, t and f are angled to match the curved terminals.</Note>
	<Note term="G, S, s">G and S wide and round, s a little leaner, with slightly open, angled ends on S and s.</Note>
	<Note term="Display">For headlines and large sizes: tracking −22, stronger kerning (115%) and compact 1.15 em line spacing.</Note>
	<Note term="UI">For buttons, cards, labels and running text: a slight +3 tracking and kerning matched to SF Pro at its text sizes, with a 1.25 em line box centred on the cap height and descenders a little deeper than Display’s. Prose sets line-height 1.5 in CSS; the extra leading falls evenly above and below.</Note>
	<Note term="Weights">Regular, Medium, SemiBold and Bold at 400, 500, 600 and 700, a little lighter than the numbers usually mean, with every weight in between.</Note>
</Notes>

Next pass: a straight-legged R and a shorter Q tail, an angled end on the j, slightly wider o, c, e and O, and a review of the accented glyphs. Licence: SIL Open Font License 1.1. Llama Sans is derived from Geist, © 2024 The Geist Project Authors.

{#if specimen.compare}
	<CompareSwitch bind:comparing={view.comparing} labels={specimen.compare.labels} />
{/if}
